# SPA fallback. Every real route is client-side.
#
# NOTE: do NOT add a public/404.html to this project. Pages then answers
# unmatched paths with it and a 404 status, which takes precedence over this
# rewrite and 404s every event page. That was tried on 2026-08-10 and took the
# site down until it was reverted.
#
# The related hazard is in _headers: a missing hashed asset falls through to
# this rule and is answered with index.html and a 200, and /assets/* is stamped
# immutable — so a browser can cache an HTML document under a .js URL for a
# year. Cloudflare's _redirects supports 200 and the 30x codes, not 404, so this
# rule cannot be narrowed to spare /assets/*; excluding them via the Pages
# Function would bill every asset request as a Worker invocation (see
# _routes.json). It is handled in the client instead — lazyPage() in
# src/main.tsx reloads the document once when a page chunk fails to import,
# which fetches a fresh index.html and with it chunk names that exist, and
# PageBoundary shows a Reload button if that did not help.
#
# THIS COMMENT ONCE DESCRIBED A GUARD THAT DID NOT EXIST. It was written as
# though main.ts already reloaded, and nothing did: on 2026-08-19 a deploy
# window served HTML for two chunks and the app rendered a blank frame with an
# uncaught TypeError. If the guard is ever removed, remove this paragraph with
